    NoCheckTrustManager.kt, ConscryptInitializer.kt, AapRead.kt,
    AapReadSingleMessage.kt, AapReadMultipleMessages.kt,
    SocketAccessoryConnection.kt and AapTransport.kt. AapTransport no longer
    builds the video path or the message handler (its session's builder passes
    the handler to startReading) and has no microphone hook; AapRead's
    factory takes the handler; each file's header lists its changes.
  - Android Auto Projection receiver, control (package aa/), from the same
    folder and commit: AapControl.kt and AapMessageHandlerType.kt, copied.
    AapControl no longer calls OpenCfMoto's AaVideoBridge, answers a
    microphone request itself (as a microphone that did not open) and builds
    the service discovery response from an AaConfig; AapMessageHandlerType is
    given the video path as a function. aa/ServiceDiscoveryResponse.kt is
    rewritten here after OpenCfMoto's file of the same name (which services
    are made of which messages, and its findings about the audio sink and the
    microphone); its header says what it keeps. aa/AaConfig.kt and the tests
    (ServiceDiscoveryResponseTest, AapControlTest) are new.
  - Android Auto Projection receiver, video (package aa/), from the same
    folder and commit: VideoDecoder.kt, copied. Its stall watchdog's rule is
    now aa/DecoderStallWatch.kt (new): the wait is measured from the first
    unanswered input after an output or a pause instead of from the last
    picture, input the phone offered counts whether or not the codec took it,
    and the watchdog begins when a decoder opens instead of with its first
    picture; the test for a hardware codec by name, for Android below 10, is
    cut. aa/AapVideo.kt is rewritten here after OpenCfMoto's file of the same
    name (the reassembly buffer, the two payload offsets, the start-code
    test, the 1 s spacing of keyframe requests) and open-headunit's (below);
    its header says what it keeps. aa/AaVideoSink.kt and the tests
    (AapVideoTest, DecoderStallWatchTest, VideoDecoderTest, AaVideoSinkTest,
    with TestH264Source) are new.
  - Android Auto Projection receiver, the link (package aa/): aa/AaReceiver.kt
    is written here after OpenCfMoto's file of the same name (same folder and
    commit). Kept: dialling out to Android Auto's head unit server on
    127.0.0.1:5277 with an 800 ms connect timeout and 2 s between dials, one
    session at a time, and the order of a session (handshake, decoder
    surface, read loop). Not here: the NSD registration, the 20-dial limit,
    BikeWifi's process-wide network bind, AaVideoBridge, the input sinks and
    the night timer (day and night are given to the receiver by the service,
    AaReceiver.setNight); the microphone has its own files, below. The
    listening socket on 127.0.0.1 for Android Auto 17.3 and older (in
    AaReceiver) and the ask that makes Android Auto dial it (aa/AaCallsIn.kt)
    are written for REau after reading OpenCfMoto's AaSelfMode; nothing of its
    code is copied. The link states and the tests
    (AaReceiverTest, AaReceiverNightTest) are new.
  - Android Auto Projection receiver, input and microphone (package aa/ and
    phone/): aa/AaInput.kt is copied from the same folder and commit (one finger
    from the phone's screen instead of multi-touch; the key codes live in
    ServiceDiscoveryResponse). aa/AaMicrophone.kt and phone/PhoneVoice.kt are
    written here after OpenCfMoto's aa/AaMicrophone.kt (same commit): the
    request/answer order, 20 ms chunks of 16 kHz mono PCM and the media data
    message are kept; the capture sits behind VoiceSource and prefers the
    Bluetooth headset. Each file's header says what it keeps and changes.
  - Thermal ceiling: stream/ThermalCeiling.kt is written here after
    AdaptivePolicy.thermalFpsCap in
    app/src/main/java/dev/zanderp/opencfmoto/AdaptiveVideoController.kt,
    commit 0abbe2a. Kept: four steps by the phone's thermal status, never
    above the base. Changed: the steps are fractions of the rider's ceiling
    (3/4, 5/8, 1/2) instead of OpenCfMoto's absolute rates; its header says
    so. ThermalCeilingTest is new.
  - res/raw/aa_cert and res/raw/aa_privkey, copied byte for byte from
    app/src/main/res/raw/ at the same commit: the head-unit certificate and
    key ("Google-Android-Reference") that open Android Auto receivers present
    in the TLS handshake. They are not this project's and not OpenCfMoto's
    work; they come down the headunit lineage.
  - The tests (AapReadTest, AapSslHandshakeTest, AapTransportTest) and the
